    escalation: 'BSOD 0x124 repetidos con RAM/disco sanos, o varios equipos del mismo modelo cayendo con el mismo código: escalar a L2/hardware — patrón de lote o fallo de placa.',
    english_customer_response_template: 'Hi {{USERNAME}}, your computer had been restarting with blue screens. We collected the crash dumps Windows saves for every stop, read the exact stop codes, and analyzed the files involved. The analysis pointed to a specific driver, which we rolled back to its previous stable version. We also ran a memory test to rule out hardware problems and it passed cleanly. Please work as usual and report any new blue screen immediately in ticket {{TICKET_ID}} — if one appears, note the code on the screen, since each code identifies a different cause. So far the machine has run stable since the fix.',
    english_technical_explanation: 'Repeated BSOD on {{HOSTNAME}} at {{TIMESTAMP}}. Minidumps collected from C:\\Windows\\Minidump; event 1001 (BugCheck) in the System log provided stop codes for crashes the user did not record. Dump analysis (BlueScreenView or WinDbg !analyze -v) identified a third-party driver as the faulting module (ntoskrnl generic, third-party driver beneath). Stop code family: 0xD1 DRIVER_IRQL_NOT_LESS_OR_EQUAL (driver). Remediation: driver rolled back via Device Manager after a Safe Mode session (shutdown /r /o). Memory verified with mdsched.exe (no errors), ruling out the 0x124 WHEA hardware path. Verification: 48 hours with no BSOD and no new event 1001.',
    keywords: ['bsod', 'pantalla azul', 'blue screen', 'stop code', 'minidump', 'bluescreenview', 'windbg', '0x7b', '0xd1', '0x124', 'whea', 'irql', 'mdsched', 'bugcheck 1001'],
