    english_customer_response_template: 'Hi {{USERNAME}}, your computer had no network because it was not receiving an IP address from the company DHCP service; Windows had assigned itself a useless automatic address instead. We confirmed the network cable and port were fine, restarted the DHCP client, and renewed the address lease. Your computer now has a valid company IP address with the correct gateway and DNS, and internet and internal systems are working again. Please test the applications you normally use. If the yellow warning icon on the network ever comes back after moving desks or docking, reply to ticket {{TICKET_ID}} before restarting anything so we can capture the status.',
    english_technical_explanation: 'No connectivity on {{HOSTNAME}}: Get-NetIPConfiguration showed an APIPA address 169.254.x.x with no default gateway at {{TIMESTAMP}}, meaning DHCP never answered. Client-side checks: DHCP Client service running, adapter Up with real LinkSpeed (physical layer healthy). ipconfig /release plus /renew did not obtain a lease. A valid static IP test with ping to the gateway succeeded, isolating the fault to DHCP delivery rather than switching. The interface was returned to DHCP with Set-NetIPInterface -Dhcp Enabled. Verification: ipconfig /all shows a corporate-range address with gateway and DNS, and the user browses normally. Single machine affected, so no infrastructure escalation required.',
