    english_customer_response_template: 'Hi {{USERNAME}}, your computer was failing to join the corporate WiFi and kept asking for credentials. We removed the saved WiFi profile so Windows negotiates the connection from scratch, verified the certificate chain used to validate the network, and synchronized the system clock, which was slightly off and breaking certificate validation. We also confirmed your account was not locked. The computer now connects automatically and stays connected. When you move between floors it may take a few seconds to reassociate, which is normal. If the prompt for credentials returns, reply to ticket {{TICKET_ID}} without changing anything so we can check the authentication logs.',
    english_technical_explanation: '802.1X authentication failure on {{HOSTNAME}} for the corporate WPA2-Enterprise SSID at {{TIMESTAMP}}. netsh wlan show profiles confirmed PEAP-MSCHAPv2 as the EAP method. Remediation: WiFi profile deleted and renegotiated cleanly, corporate root CA verified in the trusted Root store (certutil -store Root), and the system clock resynchronized with w32tm /resync because drift was breaking TLS certificate validation. Account flags checked with Get-ADUser (not locked, password valid). Verification: netsh wlan show interfaces shows connected and authenticated with a DHCP lease and no credential loop. The NPS event 6273 review was not required since only one machine was affected.',
    keywords: ['wifi', '802.1x', 'wpa enterprise', 'eap', 'peap', 'eap-tls', 'certificado', 'certificate', 'nps', 'radius', '6273', 'netsh wlan', 'w32tm', 'ssid'],
