    english_customer_response_template: 'Hi {{USERNAME}}, your network connection was fine, but web browsing was broken because your browser was using a proxy configuration that no longer matched the company standard. We compared your settings with the standard, verified the proxy service was healthy, corrected the configuration, and reset the system-level proxy to a clean state. Both internal and external sites now open, and background services like Windows Update and Teams are working again. Please test your usual sites. If you connect your laptop to other networks or use a personal VPN and browsing breaks afterwards, reply to ticket {{TICKET_ID}} so we can check the proxy settings before anything else.',
    english_technical_explanation: 'Browsing failure on {{HOSTNAME}} with healthy connectivity at {{TIMESTAMP}}. netsh winhttp show proxy showed a contaminated system proxy, and the user registry key (HKCU Internet Settings) had AutoConfigURL pointing to a stale PAC script. The corporate proxy endpoint was verified reachable on port 8080 with Test-NetConnection and the PAC URL served valid JavaScript, discarding an infrastructure outage. nslookup wpad resolved to the legitimate proxy (no WPAD spoofing). Remediation: user proxy restored to the corporate standard, WinHTTP reset with netsh winhttp reset proxy, and bypass exceptions reviewed so internal resources go direct. Verification: internal and external browsing works and Windows Update and Teams background services operate normally.',
