    english_customer_response_template: 'Hi {{USERNAME}}, your documents were stuck in the print queue and the printer showed as offline even though it was on. We confirmed the printer is reachable on the network, then restarted the Windows print service and cleared the stuck jobs stored on your computer, which is what usually blocks new jobs. After restarting the service, the queue came back empty. We also power-cycled the printer so it re-registered on the network. Please print a test page now and then the document you need; the queue should clear immediately. If the printer shows offline again tomorrow, reply to ticket {{TICKET_ID}} and we will check the printer port configuration as the next step.',
    english_technical_explanation: 'Print queue stuck and printer offline for {{USERNAME}} on {{HOSTNAME}} at {{TIMESTAMP}}. Network reachability confirmed with ping to the printer IP (reply received), discarding a network fault. Remediation: print spooler stopped cleanly, the local queue files in C:\\Windows\\System32\\spool\\PRINTERS (.SPL/.SHD) were purged, and the spooler restarted; Get-Printer then showed PrinterStatus Normal. The printer was power-cycled (30 seconds off) to re-acquire its network registration. Driver reinstall was not required in this pass. Verification: a test page printed and the queue emptied after the job. If the printer had failed ping from its own panel, or an entire floor failed against the print server, escalation to L2/hardware would apply.',
