    escalation: 'Si el re-enroll falla o el estado se pierde en varios equipos a la vez (problema del servicio o conector): escalar a M365/endpoint con el dsregcmd y las fechas de check-in.',
    english_customer_response_template: 'Hi {{USERNAME}}, your computer was marked as not compliant by the company device management service, which is why access to email and internal resources was being blocked even with correct credentials. We verified the device registration and its security token, forced a synchronization with the management service, and reviewed the compliance report: the pending Windows updates were the failing item. After the updates installed and the device checked in again, it returned to compliant status and your access is back. Please confirm you can open your email and the internal portal now. If access is ever blocked again, open the Company Portal app first and run a sync before reporting it. Reference: ticket {{TICKET_ID}}.',
    english_technical_explanation: 'Device non-compliance blocking access for {{USERNAME}} on {{HOSTNAME}} at {{TIMESTAMP}}. dsregcmd /status showed AzureAdJoined YES but AzureAdPrt NO, indicating the Primary Refresh Token had expired after a recent password change. Remediation: user signed in with the corporate account to refresh the PRT, MDM synchronization forced from Company Portal, and the compliance report read in Intune listed pending OS updates as the failing item. Updates installed and the next check-in returned the device to compliant. IntuneManagementExtension verified Running (policies and win32 apps operational). Verification: device shows Compliant, last check-in under 24 hours, and Conditional Access protected resources accessible. No re-enroll required.',
